  Abstract                 = {MRFM detection of a large slowly relaxing spin polarization induced by ESR

Lei Chen [*], Jonilyn G. Longenecker, Eric W. Moore, John A. Marohn
Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Cornell University, Ithaca, New York 14853
* Email: lei.chen@cornell.edu

The magnetic resonance force microscopy (MRFM) measurement often requires a long averaging time to achieve a good signal-to-noise ratio. Employing an enhancement technique such as dynamic nuclear polarization (DNP) could greatly speed up the detection process in MRFM. DNP has been demonstrated in conventional nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) to increase the polarization of nuclear spins by saturating the electron spin polarization in the system. Here, we detail a signal observed in an MRFM experiment that is much larger in amplitude and longer in relaxation time than our electron spin resonance (ESR) signal. A high-compliance cantilever with a 4 μm diameter magnetic tip was used to observe the spin polarization in real-time in perdeuterated polystyrene film doped with the nitroxide electron spin probe TEMPAMINE. In additional to the expected prompt cantilever frequency shift due to saturation of the nitroxide's electron-spin polarization, we also observed an associated large persistent cantilever frequency shift. This persistent signal shows the same field dependence as the ESR signal at low field. The persistent signal’s lifetime, field dependence, and size are consistent with spin polarization transferred to deuterons from electrons through cross-effect DNP.},
