  doi       = {10.1016/0039-6028(88)90325-1},
  url       = {http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0039-6028(88)90325-1},
  abstract  = {The interaction of oxygen with Ni(111) films on Re(0001), consisting of 18-50 atomic layers, was analyzed using LEED, AES and torsion oscillation magnetometry in situ in UHV (U-TOM) at room temperature. The sequence of rapid chemisorption up to &thetas;=1/3, followed by slow oxidation to 3 layers NiO, by lateral growth of islands, known from bulk crystals, has been confirmed. The initial sticking coefficient is determined to be <i>S</i><sub>O</sub>=0.52&plusmn;0.1; it persists up to &thetas;=0.2. In the chemisorption state &thetas;=1/3 a minimum <i>S</i><sub>min</sub>&les;0.005 is observed. For the initial chemisorption (&thetas;&les;0.2) the reduction of magnetic moment per O atom, &Delta;&mu;<sub>Ox</sub>, in comparison with the metallic Ni moment &mu;<sub>Ni</sub>, is given by &Delta;&mu;<sub>Ox</sub>=-4.5 &mu;<sub>Ni</sub>. It is reduced to &Delta;&mu;<sub>Ox</sub>=-3.8 &mu;<sub>Ni</sub> for &thetas;=1/3. The total reduction &Delta;<i>m</i> of the magnetic moment saturates at ~100 L with &Delta;<i>m</i>=-2.7<i>m</i><sub>ML</sub> (moment <i>m</i><sub>ML</sub> of bulk monolayer Ni), corresponding to 3 monolayers of antiferromagnetic NiO. The surface anisotropy field is reduced from 9.9&plusmn;1 T for the free surface to 0&plusmn;1 T, both for the chemisorbed and the oxidized surface},
  address   = {Netherlands},
  comment   = {Comments; jam99; 2010.06.26 = The reduction in magnetic moment due to oxygen absorbtion on Ni(111) corresponds to 3 monolayers of antiferromagnetic NiO.

Suggested filename = Elmers1988jan Magnetometric analysis of the interaction of Ni (111) with oxygen 10.1016/0039-6028(88)90325-1 *.pdf},
