@Unpublished{Marohn2005mar,
  Title                    = {Ultrasensitive Magnetic Resonance Imaging by Mechanical Detection},
  Author                   = {John A. Marohn},
  Note                     = {229th American Chemical Society National Meeting; San Diego, California; March 13 -- 17},

  Month                    = {mar},
  Year                     = {2005},

  Abstract                 = {John A. Marohn, jam99@cornell.edu1, Sean R. Garner2, Seppe Kuehn1, Jahan M. Dawlaty1, and Neil E. Jenkins1. (1) Department of Chemistry and Chemical Biology, Cornell University, 150 Baker Laboratory, Ithaca, NY 14853, (2) Department of Physics, Cornell University

We are developing a microscope that detects magnetic resonance using a microcantilever, motivated by recent suggestions that cantilever-based detection of magnetic resonance could be pushed to reach single molecule sensitivity. We have fabricated cantilevers that are sensitive to forces as small as a few attoNewtons (10^{-18} N) and have used them to detect NMR from ~10^9 gallium atoms in GaAs at high magnetic field and cryogenic temperatures. In these experiments, 71-Ga spins are selectively inverted below a magnetic tipped microcantilever, creating a spin force gradient that shifts the mechanical resonance frequency of the cantilever. While more studies of cantilever dissipation near surfaces will be required to determine whether single-proton sensitivity is achievable by cantilever detection, our experiments indicate that ~1000 proton magnetic moment sensitivity can be achieved by reducing the diameter of our tip magnets from 10 micrometer to 0.5 micrometer.

Session on:
NMR Recent Developments in Magnetic Resonance: Liquid and Solid State Applications, Theoretical Methodology, and Emerging Techniques},
