@TechReport{Sidles2011sep,
  author      = {Sidles, John A.. and Picone, Rico and Garbinia, Joeseph and Jacky, Jon},
  title       = {Transport Mechanisms for Inducting Dynamic Nuclear Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance Microsystems:{D}yanmical Theory, Design Rules, and Experimental Protocols},
  institution = {UW Quantum Systems Engineering (QSE) Group, University of Washington, Seattle, WA},
  comment     = {Suggested filename = Sidles2011sep ISH_FRIAS.pdf

From: John Sidles <sidles@u.washington.edu>
Date: September 28, 2011 5:45:49 PM EDT
To: Dan Rugar <rugar@almaden.ibm.com>, "John A. Marohn" <john.marohn@cornell.edu>, John Mamin <mamin@almaden.ibm.com>
Cc: JT Howard <jay.t.howard@gmail.com>, Alex Sidles <alexsidles@yahoo.com>, Joe Garbini <garbini@u.washington.edu>, Doug Mounce <mounce@uw.edu>, Rico Picone <piconer@u.washington.edu>
Subject: Remarks on Dan Rugar's Five Objectives for Quantum Spin Microscopy

  To: Dan Rugar, John Mamin, and the IBM Group
      John Marohn, and the Cornell Group
      JT Howard, Alex Sidles, and the FREE Group
      Joe Garbini, Rico Picone, Jon Jacky, and the UW QSE Group

From: John Sidles

  RE: Remarks on Dan Rugar's Five Objectives for Quantum Spin  
Microscopy

Dear Colleagues

We're all looking forward to getting together this week at IBM
Almaden, and I though I'd give you a preview of what I'm going
to present.  The slides I'll present on Friday are already on-line:

 URL: http://faculty.washington.edu/sidles/FRIAS_2011/index.html#Dirac-SVF
 PDF: http://faculty.washington.edu/sidles/FRIAS_2011/ISH_FRIAS.pdf

These slides are pretty much what I presented at Freiberg in July,
with the notable exception of Slide 15.a (to which the above URL is
a link).

Slide 15.a establishes the congruity of Dirac's theory of separative
isotope transport to our theory of polarization transport.  I've
worked pretty hard on this, with the narrow objective of better
understanding how DNP works in the context of MRFM, and the broader
ojjective of providing a solid roadmap for MRFM that addresses all
five of (what we in Seattle call) Rugar's Five Objectives, as Dan
articulated them at the (Washington DC) ARL in summer of 2009.

Here is our Seattle view of these cardinal objectives, given as a
two-paragraph summary of each objective

=== Begin Essay =================================================

Dan Rugar's Five Objectives for Quantum Spin Microscopy

The following five objectives for quantum spin microscopy are
presented in descending order of priority, according to our
Seattle recollection of Dan's ARL remarks.

---------------------------------------------------------------
Objective 1: Experiments that compellingly demonstrate new physics

The new physics to be demonstrated is the order-unity dynamic
polarization of nuclear spins (DNP), arising not by traditional
transfer mechanisms (e.g., the solid effect), or by optical means,
but rather by separative transport (DNP-ST).

These experiments will be the main topic of discussion,
and from a purely theoretical point of view, it's our Seattle
intention to hold nothing back, with the primary objective
that we *all* succeed in observing this effect.

No individual aspect of this new physics is hard, but there are
a *lot* of details (obviously); details that are summarized in
the slides, and which I will review in detail at Almaden.

---------------------------------------------------------------
Objective 2: Transformational practical applications

As I will discuss, there are solid reasons to expect that if DNP-ST
is observed in MRFM (that is, at the nanoscale in large field
gradients) then it can be elicited at all scales up-to-and-including
clinical MRI imaging.

At present, two new patents per week are issued that are associated
to clinical hyperpolarization applications.  This is because
everyone appreciates the following economic opportunity:

(1) raw 13C costs about $1-$10 per gram to produce (by centrifuge)
(2) this same 13C sells for about $200/gram (Cambridge Isotope  
Laboratory)

The sole reason 13C isotope separation is not *already* the most
profitable business on the planet, is that the annual global demand
for 13C saturates at around 100 kg/year, and CIL presently has
sufficient capacity on-line to meet this net global demand.  DNP-ST
has the potential to increase 13C demand to 10^3--10^4 kg/year (that
is, a 100X demand increase), and it appears that every uranium
separation consortium in the world is *already* aware of this
opportunity and preparing to exploit it.

---------------------------------------------------------------
Objective 3: Acknowledgement and respect from our peers

The Gunther Laukien Prize is of course concrete evidence of the
acknowledgement and respect for which we hoped back in 2009. Now we
have an opportunity to ascend to the next level, by setting an
agenda of experimental and theoretical investigations that will
attract a broad community of our peers to MRFM-related research.

One historical precedent, therefore, for our Almaden Meeting is Lars
Onsager's "Centrifuge Flow Theory Study Group" of the early 1960s,
which launched the modern era of isotope separation.  A broader
historical precedent is (of course) von Neumann's "Strategic Missile
Evaluation Committee (SMEC)" of 1953.

---------------------------------------------------------------
Objective 4: Solid understanding of the physics & the applications

The theoretical framework that we in Seattle have devised does not
yet have a clever name (we usually call it "quantum pullback theory"),
but it *does* have powerful implications, both for science and for
the many new enterprises that will be born of that science.

Specifically, guided by this theory, we have worked out the general
scaling behavior of DNP-ST, but in magnetic field gradients and
without, and we can foresee and forestall at least some of the
"gotchas" that surely will afflict our early experiments (as we will
discuss in Almaden).

Here too there are direct historical parallels with respect to von
Neumann's theory of shock waves in both missile and warhead design,
and with respect to the Dirac/Onsager theory of gas centrifuges in
uranium isotope separation.  In all three cases, theory helps us:
(1) conceive and debug the *first* experiments, and then (2) design
compelling *subsequent* experiments, and finally (3) launch
enterprises that *apply* the results of these experiments to solve
global problems and grasp global enterprise opportunities.

---------------------------------------------------------------
Objective 5: Public appreciation and support

Quantum systems engineering in general, and DNP-ST in particular,
point to a world in which ordinary citizens benefit as follows:

(1) Clinical-scale DNP-ST applications

    Every 5-10 years you will receive a whole-body, hyperpolarized
    MRI/MRSI scan (this requires about 5 grams of hyperpolarized 13C
    nuclei, and a low-field imaging magnet).  The doctor is watching
    for early prostate/breast/liver/lung tumors, etc, and watching
    aslo for early degenerative conditions (e.g., osteoarthritis,
    Alzheimers).  All of these conditions are diagnosed early
    and treated prophylactically (before they become acute).

(2) Cellular-scale DNP-ST applications

    Tumors, if found, are surgically removed and/or treated with
    next-generation therapeutic agents derived from submicron-scale
    MRI/MRSI imaging and spectroscopy of cell cultures.  Cell
    cultures are imaged by tabletop scale "pre-clinical" devices,
    such as those sold by Agilent and Bruker, whose resolving power
    (both spatial and spectroscopic) is transformationally enhanced
    by continuous-flow culture media that are hyperpolarized by
    liquid-phase DNP-ST (by technical means that I will discuss).

(3) Molecular-scale DNP-ST applications

    The treatments pioneered at the cellular scale, and monitored at
    the clinical scale, are designed at the molecular scale, via
    DNP-ST boosted MRFM having nanometer-scale resolution, working
    in partnership with advanced molecular dynamical simulations.
    This is (of course) the original MRFM dream, now brought
    unexpectedly close, unexpectedly fast, via the power of DNP-ST.

In aggregate, by applying DNP-ST hyperpolarization methods
simultaneously at the clinical, cellular, and molecular scales, we
can rapidly progress toward a world in which healing processes are
understood, observed, and medically controlled at every scale that
matters to people who need healing.

=== End Essay =================================================

That's all! I'll see you in Almaden on Friday!

Sincerely, John Sidles
           Professor, UW Department of Orthopaedics
           Co-Director, UW Quantum Systems Engineering Laboratory},
